<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qingzhou</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qingzhou is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, named after one of the ancient Nine Provinces and an important regional capital from the Han to the Ming. The nearby fortress of Guanggu was the capital of the Southern Yan state from about 400. In 1996 a hoard of some 400 Buddhist sculptures, many from the Northern Wei to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a> periods, was found at the site of its Longxing Temple.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qingzhou.jpg" alt="Qingzhou" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ngguls · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20211114_%E9%9D%92%E5%B7%9E%E4%B8%89%E5%AE%98%E5%BA%99.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Qingzhou lies in the west of the prefecture-level city of Weifang in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>. It was formerly Yidu County, which was abolished in 1986 and replaced by a county-level Qingzhou City. The name first appears in the Yu Gong chapter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Shujing</a>, which says that between the Sea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tai">Mount Tai</a> there lies Qingzhou, one of the ancient Nine Provinces. Because the east corresponds to the element of Wood, whose colour is qing, the area took its name. From the 2nd century BCE to the 7th century the name described a province-level region covering much of north Shandong; from the 7th century it named a prefecture centred on today's city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Spring and Autumn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States periods</a> the area belonged to the state of Qi, whose capital moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yingqiu">Yingqiu</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a>, near Qingzhou. In 204 BCE the seat of Guang County was set up in what is now the south-west of the city, and in 106 BCE the Qingzhou Inspectorate was established, headquartered at Linzi.</p><p>In 311, under the Western Jin, Cao Yi built Guanggu, which became the seat of the Qingzhou Inspector. Murong De captured it and made it the capital of Southern Yan, the only place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> to have served as a dynastic capital. Liu Yu then destroyed Southern Yan, razed Guanggu and built Dongyang City. In 412 the Buddhist pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faxian">Faxian</a> landed at Laoshan and went to Qingzhou to translate the scriptures he had collected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>The seat of Yidu County moved to Dongyang in 557. Qingzhou was a prefectural or provincial seat under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a>, Tang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, Jin and Yuan, and under the Ming and Qing the seat of Qingzhou Prefecture. From 1368 to 1377 it was the capital of Shandong Province before the seat moved to Jinan. It was also one of eleven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> garrisons of the Qing, and its soldiers fought at Zhenjiang in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Its Muslim district has 25 Hui neighbourhoods and the Zhenjiao Mosque, which dates from 1302. Qingzhou Museum shows Buddhist statues found buried in the area, thought to have been hidden because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-huizong">Huizong</a> repressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a>. The city also holds the Song pavilion Fan Gongting and the Ming garden Ou Yuan, and in 2013 the State Council designated it a national historical and cultural city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qingzhou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the war with the Mongols, the official Li Zengbo wrote in 1259 that <b>Qingzhou</b> was making one to two thousand iron-cased bombshells a month.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Yet the state was prosperous: its Code of 564 became a model for Sui and Tang law, and Buddhism flourished, as the cave temples of Xiangtangshan and the sculpture of <b>Qingzhou</b> show.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a></li><li><blockquote>In 399 he moved east to Guanggu (near <b>Qingzhou</b>, Shandong) and in 400 declared himself emperor.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-yan">Southern Yan</a></li></ul></section>
